    mainDescription: "<p>A qualified intermediary is not optional in a deferred like kind exchange. Under Treasury Regulation Section 1.1031(k)-1(g)(4), an investor in Oklahoma City, OK who wants to defer gain on the sale of relinquished property must use a qualified intermediary to hold the sale proceeds and to acquire and transfer the replacement property, because an investor who receives the sale proceeds directly, even briefly, is treated as having actual receipt of the funds, which disqualifies the exchange entirely regardless of intent. This service coordinates the qualified intermediary relationship from the exchange agreement through final disbursement, so the mechanics of the exchange do not become the reason it fails.</p><h3>What A Qualified Intermediary Actually Does</h3><p>The qualified intermediary enters into a written exchange agreement with the investor before the relinquished property closes, is assigned the investor's rights in the sale contract, receives and holds the net sale proceeds in a qualified escrow account or qualified trust account, and later uses those funds to acquire the identified replacement property and transfer it to the investor. The qualified intermediary also cannot be the investor's employee, attorney, accountant, real estate agent, or broker who has acted in that capacity within the two years before the exchange, since such a relationship would make the person a disqualified party under the regulations. We help investors in Oklahoma City, OK select an intermediary that satisfies these independence requirements and confirm the exchange agreement properly restricts the investor's right to receive, pledge, borrow, or otherwise obtain the benefit of the exchange funds before the replacement property closes.</p><h3>Coordinating Escrow, Assignment Notices, And Closing Disbursement</h3><p>Between the relinquished property closing and the replacement property closing, the qualified intermediary must issue assignment notices to both the buyer of the relinquished property and the seller of the replacement property, confirming the assignment of contract rights, and must keep exchange funds in a qualified escrow account that limits the investor's access consistent with the safe harbor rules. We coordinate directly with the qualified intermediary, the title company, and the lender to make sure assignment notices are delivered on schedule, escrow statements are structured correctly, and funds move from the qualified intermediary straight to the replacement property closing rather than through the investor. This coordination also supports the documentation an investor will later need for Form 8824, and it applies the same way whether replacement property is located in Oklahoma City, OK or acquired nationwide, since Oklahoma generally follows the federal deferral once the exchange is properly executed.</p><p>Investors selecting a qualified intermediary for the first time often ask what separates a reputable provider from a risky one, since the industry is not subject to uniform federal licensing in the way real estate brokers or attorneys are. Relevant questions generally include how the qualified intermediary segregates client funds, whether exchange proceeds are held in a qualified escrow account with the investor as a named party who must approve any disbursement, whether the intermediary carries fidelity bond coverage and errors and omissions insurance, and how long the company has operated without a history of client fund issues. We help investors in Oklahoma City, OK evaluate these factors before signing an exchange agreement, since choosing a qualified intermediary is effectively choosing who will hold the entire proceeds of the relinquished property sale for up to one hundred eighty days.</p><p>Once the qualified intermediary is selected, we also coordinate the practical handoffs that occur throughout the exchange, confirming the exchange agreement is executed and the assignment of the relinquished property sale contract is completed before that closing, tracking that the identification letter reaches the qualified intermediary inside the forty five day window, and confirming the qualified intermediary's final settlement statement matches the replacement property closing figures before funds are released.</p><p>Contact us to discuss qualified intermediary coordination for your Oklahoma City, OK exchange. We can share references upon request.</p>",
